<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phra Aphai Mani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1822–<a href="/years/1844">1844</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phra Aphai <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> is an epic verse romance by the Thai poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunthorn-phu">Sunthorn Phu</a>, composed in instalments over some two decades in the early nineteenth century. It tells of a prince who prefers the magic flute to swordsmanship and his adventures with giants, mermaids and Western sea-farers. One of the longest poems in Thai, it is a classic of Thai literature, still taught in schools.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phra-aphai-mani.jpg" alt="Phra Aphai Mani" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sunrise_on_Hat_Saikaew,_Koh_Samet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Phra Aphai Mani and his brother Sri Suwan, princes of Rattana, are sent abroad to study. Sri Suwan masters the sword, Aphai a magic flute, the pi, whose music puts listeners to sleep or kills them, and their father, finding these skills unfit for rulers, banishes them. The sea ogress Phisuea Samut carries Aphai off to her cave, where, disguised as a beautiful maiden, she bears him a son, Sin Samut. After eight years Aphai escapes with the help of a family of merfolk; the mermaid daughter takes him to Koh Kaeo Phitsadan and bears him a second son, Sud Sakhon, and in the end the flute kills the ogress.</p><p>Later books follow Aphai's marriage to Princess Suwanmali of Phareuk, the wars with Prince Usaren of Lanka and his sister Laweng, whose bewitching picture charms Aphai to her side, and the adventures of his sons, Sud Sakhon riding the dragon horse Ma Nil Mangkorn. At the close Aphai divides his lands among his heirs and becomes a monk, joined by Suwanmali and Laweng.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunthorn-phu">Sunthorn Phu</a> wrote the poem in klon suphap verse, from around 1822 or 1823 to 1844. Unlike his other works, which retell folk stories such as Khun Chang Khun Phaen, it is his own invention. He meant to end it with Aphai's abdication and retreat to the forest, which fills 64 samut Thai books, but his patron asked him to go on; the full text published by the National Library fills 132 books and more than 600,000 words. The shorter version is regarded as the authoritative text.</p><p>The poem breaks with earlier Thai verse tales by including steam-powered ships and a mechanical music player, European mercenaries and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, and places that match real coasts and islands of the Andaman Sea and the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Some Thai literary critics read the poem as an anti-colonial tale disguised as fantasy, written while European powers were expanding in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. Other Thai scholars suggest it drew on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argonauts">Argonauts</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights</a>, comparing Aphai to Orpheus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a>, the ogress to Calypso and Laweng to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helen-of-troy">Helen of Troy</a>. The story has been adapted in films such as The Adventure of Sudsakorn and in the comic series Apaimanee Saga, and statues of its characters stand on Ko Samet.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Phra Aphai Mani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Culturally the early kings rebuilt Ayutthayan court culture: the Ramakien, the Thai version of the Ramayana, was compiled under Rama I, the poet Sunthorn Phu composed <b>Phra Aphai Mani</b> in the early nineteenth century, and temples such as Wat Pho and Wat Arun gave Bangkok its skyline.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
